// CARD a6-01 · SEE: shoppers riding escalators and streaming across a busy mall, the camera sliding with them · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_busy_mall_escalators;move:right;z:.25;in:right"
{excited} What came next is the comeback everyone **remembers**.

// CARD a6-02 · SEE: a packed theatre applauding, dim; ticket stubs fly across faster and faster while the box-office total runs to $468.1M and locks · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@box_office;clip:stock_people_applause_02;to:468.1;go:took;span:2;lab:THE LEGO MOVIE · 2014;sub:worldwide box office (Box Office Mojo);out:right"
In twenty fourteen, The LEGO Movie took about four hundred and sixty-eight million dollars **worldwide**.

// CARD a6-03 · SEE: the real 2014 Irish Times page with its LEGO Movie photo dim behind; two blocks rise: $468M for the first film, then on "half" a far lower one for the sequel, about $190–200M · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@blocks;vals:468.1,195;labs:THE LEGO MOVIE · 2014,THE SEQUEL · 2019;txts:$468M,$190–200M;show:1;ats:half;bg:lego_doc_headline_irishtimes_2014;hl:1;hlat:half;in:right"
The sequel, five years later, made less than half of **that**. (J)

// CARD a6-04 · SEE: a timelapse of a LEGO Saturn V rocket rising stage by stage on a mat · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_build_saturnv;move:in;z:.2;out:right"
But here's the twist: the movies weren't the comeback. Hits come and go, and the system is what **compounds**.

// CARD a6-05 · SEE: LEGO's own results release of 10 March 2026 in a browser window, scrolling; on "reached" the line "Revenue increased 12% to DKK 83.5 billion" is highlighted · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@web_scroll;doc:lego_doc_results_2025_lego;crop:.28,.72;from:.06;to:.27;mark:.357,.377;go:reached;url:lego.com · 10 March 2026;in:right"
By twenty twenty-five, LEGO's revenue reached eighty-three and a half billion **kroner**.

// CARD a6-06 · SEE: the white LEGO House in Billund dim behind; one brick for 2005 stands on the floor; on "twelve" a stack drops in brick by brick beside it until it is 11.8 bricks tall: ×11.8 · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@growth_stack;bg:lego_lego_house_01;a:1;b:11.8;go:twelve;alab:2005 · DKK 7.05B;blab:2025 · DKK 83.5B;lab:×11.8;out:right"
That's nearly twelve times its two thousand and five **level**.

// CARD a6-07 · SEE: LEGO's headquarters in Billund dim behind; four bars of sales, one per year; on "year" each fills its operating-profit share in gold: 31.1%, 34.2%, 25.2%, 26.4% · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@slice_returns;bg:lego_hq_2024;rows:2010^31.1~2015^34.2~2024^25.2~2025^26.4;go:year;lab:OPERATING PROFIT · SHARE OF SALES;in:right"
And year after year, between a quarter and a third of its sales came back as operating **profit**.

// CARD a6-08 · MOTIF · SEE: the white LEGO House in Billund with giant bricks on its plaza; the brick drops in and clicks down onto the plaza; IT STILL FITS sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_lego_house_billund_04;go:fits;x:.4;y:.72;h:.1;lab:IT STILL FITS;lx:.5;z:.25;move:right;out:right"
{warm} So, back to that brick from nineteen fifty-eight. It still **fits**.

// CARD a6-09 · SEE: black; the thesis arrives word by word in large serif and holds dead still; "counting" and "cost" in gold · SEAM hard cut · HOLD
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@thesis_black;lines:Not a lack of ideas.~It stopped counting~what each idea cost.;go:die;span:3.4;size:84;hi:counting,cost"
LEGO didn't nearly die from a lack of ideas. It nearly died because it stopped counting what each idea **cost**.

// CARD a6-10 · SEE: Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen today, outdoors in a blue scarf, the camera pushing slowly in · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;under:lego_kjeld_2018;ua:0;fy:.05;move:in;z:.18;in:right"
And it survived because the people who owned it could afford to lend, and chose not to **sell**.

// CARD a6-11 · SEE: a girl in a toy and book shop looking up at the shelves, dim; THE LESSON; on "costs" one question card slides in: CAN ANYONE SAY WHAT EACH ONE COSTS? · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@two_questions;bg:lego_clip_toy_shop;lab:THE LESSON · BEFORE ANYTHING NEW;qs:CAN ANYONE SAY WHAT EACH ONE COSTS?^costs;out:right"
So here's the lesson: when a company you love keeps adding things, ask one question. Can anyone say what each one **costs**?

// CARD a6-12 · SEE: plastic granules pouring; on "games" the frame splits and two kids with controllers slide in, on "films" an applauding audience, on "carbon" smoke drifting; then it closes · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@footage_split;base:lego_clip_plastic_granules;clips:lego_clip_kids_game,stock_people_applause_02,stock_texture_smoke_black_14;keys:games,films,carbon"
LEGO's next test isn't plastic, because its newest pieces are games, films and promises about carbon, and none of them comes in a box you can **count**. [BEAT]

// CARD a6-13 · SEE: a LEGO-built Wimshurst machine at work, foil spheres and wires in close-up, the camera pushing in · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_build_wimshurst;move:in;z:.2;out:right"
{awe} So can a company that learned to count every single brick learn to count those too, before its catalogue grows too big all over **again**?

// CARD a6-14 · SEE: the night boardroom again (AI moment art, no faces), the camera gliding back along the table the other way · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;under:lego_scene_boardroom_2003;ua:0;move:left;z:.35;in:right"
And if you had been the family in two thousand and four, with the bankers' advice on the table, would you have sold LEGO to the highest bidder, or lent it the **money**?

// CARD a6-15 · SEE: the opening hands again, pressing two bricks together; the picture freezes on the click · SEAM in right, HOLD to the end card
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_white_bricks_hands;move:in;z:.12;freeze:keep;in:right"
Tell us in the comments, and tell us which set you'd **keep**.
